## How to read the results

This notebook compares parser output on the same set of TIL PDFs. The metrics below help you judge whether a parser is usable and what it is good at.

| Metric | What it means | How to read it |
|---|---|---|
| `parse_success` | Whether the parser completed without error | `1.0` means success, `0.0` means failure |
| `char_count` | Total characters extracted into text | Higher usually means more content was captured |
| `word_count` | Total words extracted into text | Useful as a second check on overall extraction volume |
| `required_field_hits` | Count of required-field keyword hits in extracted text | Current check counts keyword matches for `title`, `section`, `field`, `value` (regex-based and configurable) |
| `table_count` | Number of tables captured | Higher is useful when tables matter for downstream extraction |
| `formula_hits` | Count of formula-like or numeric-expression regex matches | Count is computed on combined parser output (`raw_text` + table content + elements) to keep comparison fair across parser formats; still a proxy signal, not formula correctness |
| `noise_ratio` | Fraction of repeated non-empty lines in extracted text | Computed as `duplicate_non_empty_lines / total_non_empty_lines`; lower is better (`0.00` is clean, around `0.10-0.30` means moderate repetition, `>0.30` means noisy/repetitive output) |
| `latency_s` | Time taken to parse one PDF | Lower is faster |

## Simple way to interpret parser quality

Use the metrics together, not one by one.

- Start with `parse_success`. If this is low, the parser is not usable as-is.
- Then check `char_count`, `word_count`, and `required_field_hits` to see whether the parser is capturing enough content.
- Check `table_count` if table extraction matters for your downstream workflow.
- Check `formula_hits` for TILs that contain formulas or technical calculations.
- Check `noise_ratio` to see whether the extracted text is too repetitive or messy.
- Use `latency_s` only after quality looks acceptable.


## What this notebook does not measure yet

These are still proxy metrics. They tell you whether extraction looks strong, but they do not prove every field is correct.

## What "gold labels" means

If later you create a small truth set for a few TILs, you can compare extracted fields against known correct values.

Example:
- TIL number = `2284`
- compliance category = `S`
- timing code = `4`

That truth set is what we mean by "gold labels".

If you attach those expected field values to each evaluation case, the evaluator can compute an additional metric called `field_exact_match`, which tells you how often extracted fields exactly match the known correct values.

In simple terms:
- current notebook = compares parser quality using proxy signals
- gold-label evaluation = compares parser output against known correct answers

